Paste-ready 28 SEP READ TABLE v1. Before any 28 Sep score, it fixes:
- how each W1 lever is coded: CI = exp76a/exp76c, SEC = exp76d/exp76g, DIV = exp76e/exp73m/none by the 45/46 row;
- which W2 (28 Sep late morning or afternoon), W3 and W4 (29 Sep 00:05-01:45Z) files each outcome triggers;
- the last useful submit time;
- the finals proposal rule: public-aimed = V_pub with its caveats; private-aimed = #37 per the user's letter (b).
It is the R18-A1 card made mechanical for 28-29 Sep. It amends the card in two places only: the W1 order and stagger, and allowing the single CI stack on 28 Sep. The full text is in 'action'.

A 20-min W1 stagger (R18-A7: 00:05/00:25/00:45Z) does not reliably separate completions: same-batch K2-family latencies swap order by ~20 min, and files submitted almost together co-complete.
New order:
- CI at 00:05Z (it completes ~1.5-2.5 h later anyway, because of the post-CSV CI stage);
- SEC at 00:10Z;
- DIV at 00:45Z, so the two K2-like files are >= 30 min apart.
The same >= 30 min rule applies to W2/W4. Expected completions: SEC ~07:30-08:15Z, DIV ~08:10-08:50Z, CI ~09:40-10:45Z. Cost: 0.

What gates W2 is DUMMY availability, not the reads. The Kaggle queue held exp75's dummy for ~4 h (13:19-17:20Z). If W2 dummies are pushed only after the W1 reads (~08:00-10:45Z), W2 can slip past 12:00Z, and a K2-like W2 file submitted at 12:00Z scores ~19:30-20:00Z. Two row-B stacks do not exist yet: exp76j = exp70b + cap6 + secFT and exp76k = exp76j + CI.
Proposal: once the 46 read fixes the row (~22:40Z), push speculative dummies overnight for that row's W2 column only, in this order:
- exp76f, in every row: P(CI+ and SEC+) is only ~0.1, but it is the only CI+SEC stack;
- row B: exp76i, then exp76j/exp76k (build, check the sha, diff against exp76e);
- row A: exp76h.
Cost: ~0.5-0.6 GPU-h per dummy (2 T4 x ~25-35 min), up to ~2.5 GPU-h; 0 slots.
